{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "gpt-6-sol",
  "run": 3,
  "simulation_id": "sim-03",
  "member_id": "pearce-mark",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B010",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B039",
    "B042"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B039 to B010. IceCube remains an achievement of the first order, but the unresolved question is not its scientific importance: it is whether two names can defensibly delimit decisive conception, construction, and discovery contributions within an observatory-scale collaboration. On the evidence before us, optical lattice clocks offer the more complete final configuration. The magic-wavelength lattice enabled many atoms to serve as an exceptionally precise clock without forfeiting the narrow transition, and the achievement has been independently reproduced and put to established scientific use. Katori and Ye provide a compact conceptual and experimental credit line, while the citation can remain tightly limited to lattice clocks rather than the whole history of optical-frequency metrology.",
    "case_against": "B010 must not be treated as a prize for a future redefinition of the second or for every prospective fundamental-physics application. Its historical boundary with wider optical-frequency metrology also needs exact scrutiny. Conversely, changing away from B039 should not be read as discounting new-messenger astronomy: its diffuse flux, source associations, and sustained programme establish a durable scientific opening. The present reservation is the adequacy of the proposed small laureate set, which is decisive at this final stage.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that optical lattice clocks combine a clear physical concept, decisive realization, cross-laboratory reproducibility, established scientific utility, and a compact Katori-Ye attribution.",
        "response": "I agree, especially with the distinction between demonstrated capability and future standard-setting. As an instrument builder, I regard independent performance across laboratories as strong evidence that a measurement method has become a durable physical achievement. The final citation should recognize the lattice-clock invention and development alone, so that the compact attribution does not absorb broader collective metrology."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth emphasizes that the magic-wavelength lattice made precise control of many atoms possible and that the resulting clock performance has been independently established over many years.",
        "response": "I concur that this identifies the physics that makes B010 more than an incremental improvement in timing. The demonstrated atomic-control and measurement capability is sufficient on its own; emerging applications are corroborative rather than the basis for the award. Her caution about the archival boundary between Katori and Ye's work and wider optical-frequency advances remains essential."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson accepts that IceCube opened high-energy neutrino astronomy but stresses that its appropriate individual representation is particularly difficult, while preferring the quantitatively transformative CMB achievement.",
        "response": "I agree with his diagnosis of the IceCube attribution problem and have given it greater weight in changing configuration. I retain B039 as the leading alternative because opening a new observational channel has exceptional scientific force, but cannot conclude from the supplied record that the Karle-Halzen pair captures the decisive chain fairly enough. B049 is also mature and important, yet its proposed three-person allocation likewise needs a sharper account across the extensive observational and theoretical lineage."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the priority record cleanly distinguishes Katori and Ye's optical-lattice-clock contributions from the broader development of optical frequency metrology.",
      "Whether a future review of IceCube can establish a defensible small set of laureates representing conception, construction, and discovery leadership.",
      "The appropriate relative weight of a mature enabling measurement capability and an observational opening whose individual attribution remains unresolved."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict requiring recusal is apparent. My experience in astroparticle instrumentation strengthens my appreciation of B039 but does not create a disclosed collaboration connection to the named IceCube candidates."
  }
}
